6045417] [17592186500204 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500204 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "30a9f7e5-72a7-5d8d-87b1-f07b7357b6d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500237 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8f647258-0007-5333-ac78-26a8426f4858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500299 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500299 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500299 #edb/ref 1071 "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500299 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500299 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500311 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500311 #edb/ref 1069 "q-317613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500311 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500311 #edb/ref 1071 "A video was posted on a streaming platform with $50$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the video was posted, the number of views increased by $120%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nAccording to this model, how many views are there estimated to be after $3$ hours?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500311 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500311 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=50$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after the video was posted\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=50\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{3}≈532\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $532.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-318202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500323 #edb/ref 1071 "When a new article was first published on a news website, it had $40$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after publication, the number of views increased by $190%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the article was published, and $t\\le 6?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500323 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.9$ is the growth rate ($190%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after publication\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=40\\cdot (1+1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500378 #edb/ref 1071 "A watch collector bought a watch for $$50.$ They estimate that the value of the watch increases at a rate of $8%$ every decade. According to this estimate, what will be the price of the watch after $3$ decades? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500378 #edb/ref 1074 "The price of the watch $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $8%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=50$ dollars is the original price of the watch.\n\n- $r=0.08$ is the growth rate ($8%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =50\\cdot (1+0.08)^{t} \\\\  & =50\\cdot (1.08)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function at $t=3$ gives the value of the watch after $3$ decades.\n\n$$\ny(3)=50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}≈63\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the watch after $3$ decades will be approximately $$63.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500399 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500399 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500423 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500423 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500423 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500427 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500427 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500427 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500444 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500444 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500444 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500378 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
